{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792948593,"nonce":"PEWp7t86icmdDZJ4RTXUEdICwhM=","title":"ash-channel: byte messages between a browser program and a page agent","message":"Requested by the RayDE session (RayDE git-bug ae3a156): the editor UI runs in a page, the workspace and programs in a remote session, one Cloudflare Durable Object per visitor, reached over wss:// with a token in the first message.\n\nA program in a browser runs in a Worker that never returns to its own event loop, so a WebSocket it creates (browser host, sockets.rs) cannot open or deliver while the program runs; it also builds the URL as ws://A.B.C.D:PORT/ with no hostnames, wss, subprotocol or query. The page's thread has a live event loop. ash_host_agent already starts an agent there over the program's shared memory, and ash_host_watch wakes a parked fiber, but nothing generic used them: the window and gpu agents each carry their own ring.\n\nAdded: crates/ash_hdll_channel (a threaded, position-independent wasm side module, ash_channel.wasm), haxelib/ash-channel (ash.channel.Channel: open, send, recv returning a future, close, isOpen/isClosed), js/channel-agent.mjs (the page's half: serve(ask, transport), the transport supplied by the page: open({name, deliver, closed}) -\u003e {send, close}), scripts/build_wasm_channel.sh, and examples/ring-echo (Haxe client, echo transport, build.sh, run-chrome.mjs).\n\nProtocol: one 128-byte header plus two single-writer rings in shared memory; record header kind | (length \u003c\u003c 8) (24-bit length, so a frame can exceed 64 KiB, unlike the window agent's 16-bit one); a message payload is a u32 byte length then the bytes; head/tail published with atomics; the page adds one to the program's wake word and notifies it after publishing.\n\nTested in headless Chrome (examples/ring-echo/run-chrome.mjs): 433 frames including 400 of varied size that wrap 8 KiB rings, 30 pipelined, the largest allowed frame, a frame one byte over refused, a recv that parks and is woken after ~6 ms against a 5 ms transport delay, recv after close throws ChannelClosed. Works with and without ASH_WASM_FIBERS=1. 6 ring unit tests run on the host.\n\nNeeds the shared-memory build (wasm32-wasip1-threads) on a cross-origin-isolated page (ash serve sends both headers).\n\nFound on the way: a native exported from a side module must be the DEFINE_PRIM resolver (hlp_\u003cname\u003e(sign) returning the function), not the function; exporting the function itself traps at program start with 'function signature mismatch' before main runs. docs/wasm/hdlls.md said a Rust library for the threads target does not link as a side module; it does, with its own errno slot and --shared-memory (hlwgpu's recipe), and the note is corrected.\n\nNot done: no wss/hostname support in the built-in socket host (the channel replaces the need); no CI job for the Chrome test (it needs a browser); unload; more than the agent name as a way to pick a transport (globalThis.ashChannels or a hand-written shim).","files":null}]}